"\n## Linux DRM - Core Initialization and Driver Registration\n\n### Introduction\n| Part | Topic |\n|---|---|\n| Part 1 | Architecture and Overview |\n\nIn Part 1, we examined the structure of DRM (drm_driver → drm_device → drm_minor → drm_file) and device nodes. In this post, we will trace how this structure is created in code across two stages: the DRM core reserving major number 226 at boot time, and a driver (here, the virtual driver vkms) creating the device and registering /dev/dri/card0 and renderD128.  \n\n\n\n\n### Code Flow 1: DRM Core Initialization\nDRM is initialized once when the kernel boots. This is where major number 226, which we saw earlier, gets registered.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L1236-L1272 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndrm_sysfs_init() creates the /sys/class/drm class, and register_chrdev(DRM_MAJOR, \"drm\", &drm_stub_fops) creates the 226 drm line in /proc/devices. At this point, no device exists yet. DRM has merely reserved the entire major number 226 and registered a single \"stub\" file operations structure that intercepts every open().  \n\n### Code Flow 2: The Driver Registers the Device\nNow it is time for the driver to step in. Let's look at vkms. First, here is the drm_driver defined by the driver.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/vkms/vkms_drv.c L92-L102 (Linux v6.19)\n\nDRIVER_MODESET | DRIVER_ATOMIC | DRIVER_GEM and fops created via DEFINE_DRM_GEM_FOPS are all it takes. The function that registers this driver is vkms_create().  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/vkms/vkms_drv.c L159-L220 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\n\n\n\n#### Step 1: Allocation and Initialization (drm_dev_init)\ndevm_drm_dev_alloc() invokes drm_dev_init() via internal devm_drm_dev_init(). Here, it creates minors based on driver_features and initializes GEM.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L701-L796 (Linux v6.19)\n\nIf DRIVER_RENDER is set, it allocates a render minor, and it always allocates a primary minor. drm_minor_alloc(), which allocates a minor, picks a minor number (xa_alloc) and prepares the struct device (kdev) for sysfs.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L142-L172 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\n\n\n#### Step 2: Registration (drm_dev_register)\nOnce the driver has prepared all KMS objects, it calls drm_dev_register(). From this point onward, userspace can open the device.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L1056-L1126 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndrm_minor_register() registers the device with sysfs via device_add() and populates the minor number table (xa_store). Later, open() uses this table to look up the minor.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L174-L212 (Linux v6.19)\n\n### Code Locations\n| Step | Function | Location (Linux v6.19) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Boot: DRM core initialization (Register major number 226) | drm_core_init() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c:1236 |\n| Boot: Stub open (Swap fops) | drm_stub_open() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c:1188 |\n| Driver: Device allocation and minor creation | drm_dev_init() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c:701 |\n| Driver: Minor number and sysfs device | drm_minor_alloc() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c:142 |\n| Driver: card%d / renderD%d naming | drm_sysfs_minor_alloc() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysfs.c:551 |\n| Driver: Registration (Create /dev/dri nodes) | drm_dev_register() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c:1056 |\n| Driver: Register minor with sysfs | drm_minor_register() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c:174 |\n\n\n\n\n### Summary\nHere is what we covered in this post:  \n\n- Core initialization: drm_core_init() registers major number 226 and sets up stub fops to intercept all open() calls.\n- Driver registration: Through devm_drm_dev_alloc() → drm_dev_init() (minor number allocation) → drm_dev_register() → drm_minor_register() → device_add(), /dev/dri/card0 and renderD128 are created.\n\nNow that the nodes are available, it's time for userspace to make use of them. Next, we will explore how open() and ioctl() are handled inside the kernel."